is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch0
diagnosis_text:
Router0 is not down. Its LAN interfaces are up and it is reachable from other departments: cs_lab1 <-> router0 pings succeed, and router0’s routing table and interfaces (192.168.1.1 on eth0, 192.168.2.1 on eth1) are operational. The outage is confined to the IT Department’s Layer-2 switching.

Mechanism: IT hosts and router0 cannot resolve each other’s MAC addresses on 192.168.1.0/24. From it_lab1, ping to 192.168.1.1 returns “Destination Host Unreachable” and ip neigh shows “192.168.1.1 FAILED.” From router0, ping to 192.168.1.3 returns the same and ip neigh shows “192.168.1.3 FAILED.” This is classic ARP failure due to no L2 frame forwarding on the segment.

Localization: On the IT access switch (switch0), the bridge interface br0 is DOWN while both member ports are UP and enslaved to br0:
- switch0: br0 state DOWN; eth0 and eth1 are UP and master br0.
- router0 eth0 and all IT hosts’ eth0 are UP with correct IPs, but isolated.

Root cause: The Layer-2 bridge br0 on switch0 is administratively down/misconfigured, preventing any forwarding between router0’s uplink (link_r0_sw0) and the IT LAN ports (lan_it).

Corrective fix: On switch0, bring the bridge up and ensure it autostarts:
- ip link set br0 up
- or restart networking/bridge service per platform (e.g., systemctl restart networking)
Then verify bridging state (bridge link, brctl/bridge fdb) and ARP resolution from an IT host to 192.168.1.1. After br0 is up, IT hosts should regain gateway reachability and off-subnet access.
=== END ===